x87 inline mode with the precision flag already sticky drops the inexact
bookkeeping: no residuals, no read-modify-write of fpus per instruction.

Patch 06's shadow doubles are exact, PE included, and at PC=24 -- what
Direct3D sets, and what every 3D title of the era runs its x87 code in --
that exactness costs most of each instruction. A `fmuls m32` computed its
product, a fused residual (fmsub) to know whether the double product was
exact, rounded to 24 bits and back, xor'ed the two to know whether the
rounding was, and then loaded env->fpus, or'ed PE in and stored it back;
an add or subtract ran a five-operation TwoSum for the same bit. PE is
sticky and, in inline mode, masked: once it is set, none of that can
change anything a guest can see. In 3DMark 99's first-person test the
x87 memory forms were ~400 host bytes each (`fsubs (%edx)` 412,
`fmuls -0x30(%ebp)` 407) and a quarter of the frame, between MAX-FX's
lighting code in 3DMARK.EXE and its Pentium III DLL.

TB_FLAG_X87_PE (bit 2 of the TB flags, no hflags bit) is set when the x87
mode is inline and fpus has PE; a TB translated with it emits no
residuals (x87s_binop, fsqrt) and no x87s_set_pe at all. A chained TB is
not looked up again, so the guard checks fpus.PE at run time as well as
the mode byte, and loadvm, a debugger write or a reset are caught there.
Within one TB, fclex, fninit, fldenv, frstor and fnsave -- the five that
can clear PE -- turn the sticky mode off for the rest of the TB, which is
then exact again (fxrstor and xrstor already end theirs); ending the TB
there would have cost a main-loop exit for nothing.

Patch 20's inline lookup builds the TB flags as TCG ops, the way
cpu_get_tb_cpu_state does, and it gains the same term. A first version
did not: every indirect jump into a sticky TB missed the jump cache and
left through the epilogue -- in 3DMark's first-person test dispatch went
from 5.6 % to 14.7 % of the frame and the test lost a quarter of its frame
rate while every x87 instruction had got cheaper (343 -> 266 host bytes),
with the guard exit count at 0. Whatever next adds a TB flag has two
places to add it.

`info registers` counts the guard's exits (`x87-fast guard exits`): a
count that climbs during a title means its PE is being cleared and set in
turn, and the sticky TBs are paying for it. A TB
translated before PE was set stays exact, and slower, until the next
lookup of its pc (the next timer interrupt, at the latest).

Also: a float32 memory operand, once the existing check has said it is
zero or normal, is converted by the host's own cvtss2sd (exact for both,
signed zero included) instead of being assembled from its fields.

`-d out_asm` of `fmuls -0x30(%ebp)` in 3DMARK.EXE before this patch,
about 95 host instructions: the TLB check and load (14) and two spills,
the float32 check (13) and its assembly into a binary64 (11), the
multiply (3), the result's window check (14), vfmsub for the residual
(3), the PC=24 round trip (4), the xor/or/setne and the fpus
read-modify-write (12), and the FPU instruction/data pointer stores (8).
With PE sticky the residual and the fpus update are gone and the
assembly is a zero-extend and a vcvtss2sd: about 62.

Not addressed: the FIP/FDP stores every x87 instruction makes (only
fnstenv / fnsave / fxsave read them), the window checks and the PC=24
rounding -- exact semantics need them; a relaxed mode could drop them.

Measured on 3DMark 99 Max, claude98, 800x600x16, after patch 36: CPU
3DMarks 13549 -> 14690 (+8 %), the first-person test 15.4 -> 16.2 fps
(3DMarks 5912 -> 5917: the race tests sit at the 60 Hz flip cap). x87
battery 382,251 lines identical on/off, its PC=24 bench 0.49 -> 0.38 s;
SSE, SMC and rep batteries unchanged; scripts/test.sh all 57/57.

Behind x87-fast, like the rest of patch 06: off, none of this exists.

